### Example 1 — Insurance Claims Triage Platform

**Scope summary**
A web platform for first-line claims adjusters to intake motor insurance claims, attach
evidence photos, run automated fraud signals, and route cases to specialist teams. Integrates
with the insurer's legacy policy API (read-only) and document archive. Must support 200
concurrent adjusters and retain audit logs for seven years.

**Effort and cost**
The programme opens with three weeks of discovery: adjuster shadowing, fraud-rule workshops,
and a non-functional requirements pass covering peak-load assumptions and retention policy.
Design spans adjuster inbox, claim detail with timeline, and supervisor dashboards—about 60
hours of UX and UI work at designer rates.

Backend development dominates the budget. Policy lookup caching, claim state machine with
eight statuses, evidence upload to WORM storage, and a rules service evaluating ten fraud
heuristics together account for roughly 180 hours. The automated signals re-use an existing
scoring library but need wrapping, batch replay for backtesting, and threshold tuning with
the fraud team. Frontend delivery covers responsive adjuster workflows, bulk actions, and
accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA) across 14 screens—about 120 hours.

Integration testing against the legacy policy API sandbox, performance testing at 200
virtual users, and security review remediation add 80 hours. Deployment uses blue-green
releases with runbooks and adjuster training sessions. Total effort lands near 480 hours,
approximately 29,500 EUR blended across development and design.

**Team and timeline**
A backend lead, one full-stack developer, one frontend developer, a part-time designer, and
a QA engineer in the final four weeks. Calendar duration is 18 weeks including two weeks of
parallel UAT with a 30-adjuster pilot group.

**Risks and assumptions**
Assumes legacy policy API documentation is accurate; prior projects showed 15% field mismatch
risk. Fraud model thresholds are client-owned—engineering delivers plumbing only. Photo EXIF
tampering detection is out of scope for v1. Contingency of two weeks is recommended if
archive integration requires custom CMIS adapters.

